WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.890 --> 00:00:05.509
Sometimes all it takes is one terrifying moment
to elevate a horror movie from flash-in-the-pan

00:00:05.509 --> 00:00:07.759
fun to a must-see movie.

00:00:07.759 --> 00:00:11.920
A single scene can make the difference between
a movie that comes and goes without much fanfare

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:14.660
and one that makes its way into cinematic
history.

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:18.840
These films managed to make moments that are
not only unforgettable, but ones which viewers

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:22.029
might not be so eager to revisit.

00:00:22.029 --> 00:00:23.029
Elevator down

00:00:23.029 --> 00:00:28.269
Ti West's modern horror masterpiece, The House
of the Devil, is a quiet, tense film—right

00:00:28.269 --> 00:00:29.949
up until its infamous ending.

00:00:29.949 --> 00:00:33.960
The movie surrounds Samantha, a babysitter
who reluctantly agrees to keep an eye on a

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:35.539
couple's bedridden mother.

00:00:35.539 --> 00:00:37.750
Predictably, the night doesn't quite go as
planned.

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:41.739
But while the film as a whole is suspenseful
and masterful in its building of tension,

00:00:41.739 --> 00:00:46.399
it's the finale that makes it all pay off:
a spectacularly terrifying satanic ritual

00:00:46.399 --> 00:00:51.359
and chase scene featuring blood, guts and
the reveal of a horrifically deformed grandmother,

00:00:51.359 --> 00:00:53.969
the matriarch of a devil-worshipping cult.

00:00:53.969 --> 00:00:55.370
The end is relentless.

00:00:55.370 --> 00:00:59.110
It hits the viewer like a freight train and
doesn't slow its momentum for a second.

00:00:59.110 --> 00:01:03.620
You'll be out of breath by the time it's over
and likely not anxious to revisit it any time

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:04.620
soon.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:05.880
A close shave

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:11.250
Eli Roth tends to excel at nausea-inducing
moments, largely involving gore and mutilation,

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:14.780
and while his filmography is chock-full of
gut-churning nastiness, there's something

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:18.580
about the brutal simplicity of his debut film,
Cabin Fever.

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:21.500
The scene in question involves one of the
characters shaving her legs.

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:22.500
The catch?

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:26.079
Well, the plot of the movie revolves around
some 20-somethings in the woods who are exposed

00:01:26.079 --> 00:01:27.740
to a flesh-eating virus.

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:32.580
So as she shaves her legs, her flesh is peeling
from the bone, piece by piece.

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:37.420
It's both repulsive and enthralling, and you'll
probably never want to watch it again.

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:38.479
Discovering the tapes

00:01:38.479 --> 00:01:42.509
Few scary movies have utilized sound scares
quite as well as the psychological horror

00:01:42.509 --> 00:01:44.550
juggernaut Session 9.

00:01:44.550 --> 00:01:48.310
The film takes place in an abandoned mental
asylum and shows how the lingering spirits

00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:51.420
affect a group of men who are there to refurbish
the building.

00:01:51.420 --> 00:01:55.250
One of the men, Mike, discovers a cache of
tape recordings of therapy sessions with a

00:01:55.250 --> 00:02:00.270
patient suffering from dissociative identity
disorder, who also might also be possessed.

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:03.950
These tapes play throughout the film, but
it's that first scene that stands out as the

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:05.320
film's creepiest moment.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:09.509
The squeal of the tape, the moment the voices
appear, everything about it is masterfully

00:02:09.509 --> 00:02:10.509
executed.

00:02:10.509 --> 00:02:14.190
As soon as the voices start, the viewer knows
something is wrong.

00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:18.260
Something bad is going to happen as the sessions
move closer and closer to the titular session

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:19.260
nine.

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:20.720
"Mary?"

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:24.940
"Have you seen the doll Mr. Doctor?"

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:26.510
The scene doesn't jump out at you.

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:28.550
It doesn't feature a gory dismemberment.

00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:32.800
But it's creepy to the point that viewers
will want to avoid it upon any future revisiting

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:34.370
of the film.

00:02:34.370 --> 00:02:35.390
The sloth

00:02:35.390 --> 00:02:39.989
David Fincher's Se7en features a number of
gruesome mutilations, but choosing its standout

00:02:39.989 --> 00:02:42.930
doesn't feature dismemberment or decapitation.

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:45.380
It simply features a man left to rot.

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:49.160
The film's villain had chained the victim
for a year and simply never let him move,

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:53.560
doing just enough to keep him alive for a
long enough time to allow his body to waste

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:54.560
away.

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:58.220
The makeup is horrifying, creating an image
of a man who looks like something other than

00:02:58.220 --> 00:02:59.220
human.

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:02.210
It's a nauseating sight to behold, which is
exactly what it should be.

00:03:02.210 --> 00:03:06.299
And it's all capped off with the reveal that
the victim is still alive.

00:03:06.299 --> 00:03:10.780
In a film full of horrific imagery and dark
plot turns, the Sloth victim is the hardest

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:12.390
moment to endure more than once.

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:15.190
It doesn't have the tense narrative turns
of the film's grim ending.

00:03:15.190 --> 00:03:16.510
It doesn't need to.

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:19.460
It's horrifying enough on premise alone.

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:20.460
Night vision

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:24.610
The Descent is an exercise in unrelenting
horror, and while the whole film may be difficult

00:03:24.610 --> 00:03:29.010
to dive into more than once, the night vision
scene in particular is almost too terrifying

00:03:29.010 --> 00:03:30.160
to screen twice.

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:33.560
The film features a group of friends on a
caving trip who find themselves hunted by

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:35.480
a group of cave-dwelling mutants.

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:39.040
The film does a great job of keeping the creatures
hidden until the perfect moment.

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:42.909
Already thriving on claustrophobia and fear
of the dark, the film switches to the point

00:03:42.909 --> 00:03:46.540
of view of one of the women as she uses her
camcorder's night vision feature.

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:50.319
Her gaze jolts back and forth between her
friends, when suddenly one of the creatures

00:03:50.319 --> 00:03:52.909
is visible in plain sight behind one of them.

00:03:52.909 --> 00:03:57.799
It's a perfect jump-scare and ratchets the
fear present in the film exponentially.

00:03:57.799 --> 00:03:58.799
Home invasion

00:03:58.799 --> 00:04:03.709
Few modern horror films have been as acclaimed
as It Follows, a tense film about a girl being

00:04:03.709 --> 00:04:07.579
relentlessly pursued by a creature that takes
the form of other humans.

00:04:07.579 --> 00:04:11.989
It's a brilliantly simple premise for a horror
film and it works extremely well, but the

00:04:11.989 --> 00:04:15.409
home invasion sequence is easily the film's
standout moment.

00:04:15.409 --> 00:04:20.070
The creature, which can only be seen by protagonist
Jay, arrives at Jay's house, where she and

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:23.420
her friends are posted up in hopes of capitalizing
on strength in numbers.

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:27.790
As the creature appears, she springs into
a panic, locking herself in her room, only

00:04:27.790 --> 00:04:32.570
to soon be joined by her friends…and an
unusually tall man behind them.

00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:36.310
It's found her, and the image it takes this
time is one of the most disturbing forms it

00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:37.660
takes in the film.

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:39.250
'We all float'

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:45.040
Before it was the unstoppable box office force
of 2017, It was a lower-budget affair showcasing

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:49.270
a tour-de-force performance by Tim Curry as
Pennywise the Dancing Clown.

00:04:49.270 --> 00:04:53.250
The remake is an exceptional piece of horror,
but when comparing how each film adapted the

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:57.420
most iconic moment from the novel they're
both based on, the original is head and shoulders

00:04:57.420 --> 00:04:58.990
above the new version.

00:04:58.990 --> 00:05:00.860
It comes down to Curry's performance.

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:05.000
"Aren't you going to say hello?"

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:09.080
He takes Pennywise's demeanor in a direction
that somehow feels even fresher today than

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:11.100
it did in 1990.

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:14.600
Everything he says feels so surreally out
of place that the audience can't help but

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:16.470
feel creeped out.

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:20.340
"They float, Georgie.

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:22.910
They float."

00:05:22.910 --> 00:05:27.700
It's all tied together with the infamous final
shot of Pennywise's true form seeping through,

00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:30.080
yellowed fangs and inhuman eyes.

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:34.390
It created a generation of kids who are still
afraid of clowns to this day, and this scene

00:05:34.390 --> 00:05:35.800
is what started it all.

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:37.630
Thanks for watching!

